// `UInt64` is DECLARED to reach 18446744073709551615, and E024's own hint used
// to recommend it as the fix for a magnitude that does not fit `Int`. The
// compiler cannot represent that band: the IR carries every integer in one i64
// and MIR — the default path for both targets — knows only `NTy::I64`, so above
// `i64::MAX` division is a signed divide, `uint64.to_string` prints signed, and
// BOTH targets agree on the same wrong answer (which is why the cross-target
// fuzzer never saw it). Recommending it printed `0|-1|-1` where the program
// meant `9223372036854775807|5|18446744073709551615`.
//
// A wall is never a miscompile: until the unsigned lane exists (#872) the
// literal is rejected, not silently carried.
fn main() -> Unit = {
  let big: UInt64 = 18446744073709551615
  println(uint64.to_string(big / 2))
}
